From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: N1-CoreELEC project <dev@n1.local>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:50:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] media: amlogic: add TV-led Dolby Vision metadata injection

TV-led Dolby Vision for SoCs without a licensed Dolby Vision core
(Phicomm N1 / Amlogic GXL).

The box does not reshape the picture.  Instead the Dolby Vision
Display Management (DM) metadata is packetised into 128 byte Dolby
Vision metadata packets (CRC32 in the last four bytes), every bit of
a packet is turned into one picture pixel, and those pixels are
written into the top rows of the OSD/GUI canvas.  A Dolby VSIF packet
is sent alongside, so the TV knows that the signal carries Dolby
Vision metadata and performs the reshaping itself.

Neither the licensed DV Core hardware nor the dovi.ko blob is
needed, so this works on GXL where the DV Core registers are not
even assigned.

The DM metadata bytes are handed over from user space through the
"dv_metadata" module parameter (hex string); Kodi links libdovi and
can extract the RPU/DM block from the stream.

The packetisation, CRC and bit-to-pixel encoding follow the publicly
published reference implementation
(doppingkoala/common_drivers, drivers/media/video_sink/dv_injection.c),
adapted to the 4.9 tree: the VSIF is sent with the existing
vinfo->vout_device->fresh_tx_vsif_pkt() and the metadata is written
into the OSD canvas obtained with canvas_read().

The feature is compiled in but stays inert until
"dv_injection_enable" is set, so other devices are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: N1-CoreELEC project <dev@n1.local>
